course: dictionary
lesson: 5946
title: Finding the meaning of mast
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A mast is a tall pole. On a boat, the mast holds the sails, and sailors climb it. Read the words around the word to check. «The sailors climbed the mast to fix the sails» tells you the mast is high up on the ship, and the sails hang from it.
explain: Masts are not only on ships. «A phone mast has antennas that send and receive radio signals from phones in its area» shows that a mast can be a tall tower that carries equipment for phones. The words antennas, radio and phones are your clues.
explain: Swap in your meaning to test it. «It sends the numbers by invisible radio waves to the nearest phone mast, often a pole on a roof» says the mast is a pole. Pole fits, so your guess works.
explain: Some phrases use mast in special ways. «Flag at half mast» means the flag is lowered a little to show sadness. When a phrase does not make sense word by word, think about what the whole phrase means.
fact: A mast is a tall pole or tower.
fact: On a boat, the mast holds the sails.
fact: A phone mast has antennas that send and receive radio signals.
fact: «Flag at half mast» means a flag lowered a little to show sadness.
fact: «Nail your colours to the mast» means to say firmly what you believe.
exercise: «The sailors climbed the mast to fix the sails.» Where is the mast? | Under a bridge | In a cave | Inside a school bag | *On a ship
exercise: «Phone masts carry mobile signals.» What does a phone mast carry? | *Mobile signals | Sails for boats | Water for the town | Letters to the post office
exercise: «The flag flew at half mast.» What does half mast show? | Hunger | A birthday party | Winning a race | *Sadness
test: Uncle says, «She nailed her colours to the mast.» What does it mean? | *She said firmly what she believed | She painted a flag on a pole | She lost her coat in the rain | She climbed a tall pole
test: «A tower crane has a tall mast and a long arm.» What is the mast? | The heavy concrete weight | The cable that holds the load | The long arm that swings the load | *The tall upright part of the crane
exam: Jayden says, «A mast is the sail on a boat.» Is he right? | Yes, and it is also a flag | *No, the mast is the tall pole that holds the sails | No, a mast is a kind of fish | Yes, the sail is the mast
